{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["וָאֶתְנַפַּל לִפְנֵי יְהוָה אַרְבָּעִים וגו׳ וָאֶתְפַּלֵּל אֶל־יְהוָה וָאֹמַר וגו׳ — who is the wise man who will set his mind to why it was necessary to repeat here the fall of forty days?! Is it the way of Scripture to repeat a matter and say, ״Know that when I fell before YHWH forty days, thus and so I prayed״ — above it should have stated it, and he should not have needed to go back and repeat in order to inform Israel in this language ״thus I prayed״? But there is great wisdom here! Lest you say: now, a great sin in the matter of the calf — Moshe's prayer availed and we were saved; so too in the land of Israel, if we sin, will the prayers of the prophets avail us? Moshe said to them: prayers will not avail you in the land of Israel, for now it was atoned for you so that His name not be profaned, for thus I prayed: ״זְכֹר לַעֲבָדֶיךָ״ and so on, ״פֶּן־יֹאמְרוּ הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתָנוּ מִשָּׁם מִבְּלִי יְכֹלֶת יְהוָה לְהָבִיאָם״ — and therefore you were not liable to death on its account. But after He kills thirty-one kings and grants you possession of the land — then He will bring you out and drive you from the land, for there is no profanation of the Name there, so as to say to the nations ״from inability of YHWH״; rather, the nations will say: Israel sinned against Him. As is explicit in אַתֶּם נִצָּבִים: ״וְאָמְרוּ כָּל־הַגּוֹיִם עַל־מֶה עָשָׂה יְהוָה כָּכָה לָאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת וּמֶה חֲרִי הָאַף״ and so on, ״וְאָמְרוּ עַל אֲשֶׁר עָזְבוּ אֶת־בְּרִית יְהוָה אֱלֹהֵי אֲבֹתָם״ and so on, ״וַיִּתְּשֵׁם יְהוָה מֵעַל אַדְמָתָם בְּאַף וּבְחֵמָה וּבְקֶצֶף גָּדוֹל וַיַּשְׁלִכֵם אֶל־אֶרֶץ אַחֶרֶת כַּיּוֹם הַזֶּה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The first sentence is textually uncertain: the manuscripts oscillate between framing this as a rhetorical question about repetition and as a direct challenge to the reader. Translated hyper-literally to preserve the running syntax."}, :source-witnesse